WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.030
The overall average hides
a much harder slice.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:09.090
We defined working day rush
as hours seven, eight, nine,

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.070
sixteen, seventeen and eighteen
before opening the final outcomes.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:18.030
It contains seven hundred
and forty one recorded hours,

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:22.510
with group mean absolute error two
hundred and sixty five point four three.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:27.030
Signed error there is negative two
hundred and forty three point eight one.

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:31.310
The forest is lower at two hundred
and twenty six point seven four,

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.690
but still misses substantially.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.530
These counts and definitions
matter to interpretation.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:43.300
Finding a busy hour failure does not
establish a new causal model or an

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.250
operational inventory policy.

00:00:47.602 --> 00:00:50.332
Month slices show
different later behavior.

00:00:51.022 --> 00:00:55.739
July has seven hundred and forty three
observations and mean absolute error one

00:00:55.739 --> 00:00:57.772
hundred and thirty two point one four.

00:00:58.422 --> 00:01:02.991
September has seven hundred and twenty
and error one hundred and sixty four point

00:01:02.991 --> 00:01:03.892
three four.

00:01:04.682 --> 00:01:07.772
October has seven hundred
and eight recorded hours;

00:01:08.422 --> 00:01:12.372
December has seven hundred and
forty two and lower average error.

00:01:13.062 --> 00:01:17.012
All six observed month counts
reconstruct the final population.

00:01:17.922 --> 00:01:20.572
January through June
final slices are empty,

00:01:20.922 --> 00:01:24.592
with undefined metrics rather
than invented zero error.

00:01:25.282 --> 00:01:27.372
Month and rush dimensions overlap,

00:01:27.722 --> 00:01:30.832
so adding their separate counts
would double count observations.

00:01:33.233 --> 00:01:37.793
The total underprediction deficit is
five hundred and seventeen thousand,

00:01:37.793 --> 00:01:42.403
nine hundred and forty one point
zero zero eight five rentals.

00:01:42.493 --> 00:01:47.443
Divide by all four thousand, three
hundred and seventy five observed hours

00:01:47.933 --> 00:01:51.443
and the average deficit is one
hundred and eighteen point three nine.

00:01:52.053 --> 00:01:54.466
Divide by only the three thousand,

00:01:54.466 --> 00:01:58.867
seven hundred and thirty five
underpredicted hours and it becomes one

00:01:58.867 --> 00:02:01.003
hundred and thirty eight point six seven.

00:02:01.753 --> 00:02:05.803
Both values are correct,
with different questions and denominators.

00:02:06.333 --> 00:02:09.003
They measure misses
against observed rentals,

00:02:09.273 --> 00:02:11.503
not every person who wanted a bicycle;

00:02:12.193 --> 00:02:14.883
unmet demand is not
directly recorded here.

00:02:16.983 --> 00:02:19.693
Between the first and
last final observations,

00:02:20.183 --> 00:02:24.093
forty expected naive hourly
slots are unrecorded.

00:02:24.663 --> 00:02:27.273
Their absence does not
establish zero rentals,

00:02:27.543 --> 00:02:30.953
nor does it establish a model
error of any known size.

00:02:31.563 --> 00:02:35.273
We retain the missingness and
score only recorded outcomes.

00:02:35.783 --> 00:02:40.423
October therefore has seven hundred
and eight observed hours rather than a

00:02:40.423 --> 00:02:42.653
complete calendar month of hourly labels.

00:02:43.283 --> 00:02:47.323
Substituting zeros would change
both numerator and denominator,

00:02:47.323 --> 00:02:50.493
answering a different question
without supporting observations.

00:02:51.463 --> 00:02:53.953
The replay application
follows the same principle:

00:02:54.463 --> 00:02:59.303
an estimate may exist for a valid request
while its actual count and residual remain

00:02:59.303 --> 00:02:59.993
unavailable.

00:03:02.233 --> 00:03:07.063
The largest retained group miss occurs
on September twelfth at eighteen.

00:03:07.753 --> 00:03:11.263
The model predicts three hundred
and sixty seven point four five,

00:03:11.813 --> 00:03:14.283
while the observed count is
nine hundred and seventy seven.

00:03:15.653 --> 00:03:19.723
Its residual is negative six
hundred and nine point five five.

00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:22.663
Across the final period,

00:03:22.873 --> 00:03:26.543
mean target is two hundred
and fifty four point one one,

00:03:26.893 --> 00:03:31.023
while mean forecast is one hundred
and forty two point seven six.

00:03:31.773 --> 00:03:34.443
Their difference agrees with signed error.

00:03:34.913 --> 00:03:39.123
This describes a level gap consistent
with old fitted group means.

00:03:39.373 --> 00:03:44.643
It does not identify why demand changed
or establish a causal intervention.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.990
Nearby hours also tend to miss together.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:51.333
Over four thousand,

00:03:51.333 --> 00:03:55.010
three hundred and sixty nine
consecutive observed hour pairs,

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:58.830
residual correlation is about
zero point eight three four nine.

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:04.150
Pairs spanning missing hours are
excluded from that adjacency calculation.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:09.050
Daily and Monday start weekly
summaries retain their actual counts.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:14.480
The report does not pretend that thousands
of adjacent hours are independent future

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:15.390
experiments.

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:20.260
Its time and slice comparisons are
descriptive evidence about this one later

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:24.770
period, without an unsupported
independent hour confidence interval.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:29.720
A different future operating environment
remains an unanswered generalization

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:33.770
question rather than something
a large row count guarantees.

